    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>I, you, we, they</strong> + the base verb. <strong>He, she, it</strong> + verb <strong>+ -s</strong>.<br><em>I work. You work. He work<strong>s</strong>. We work. They work.</em><br>A name or a singular noun is <em>he/she/it</em>: <em>Amira works. My brother works.</em> Two or more people are <em>they</em>: <em>My parents work.</em><br>Common mistake: ✗ <em>He work</em> → ✓ <em>He works</em>.",
        "tag": "The -s"
      },
      {
        "html": "Some verbs need <strong>-es</strong> or <strong>-ies</strong> for he/she/it.<br>After <em>-ch, -sh, -s, -x</em> and <em>-o</em>: <em>watch → watch<strong>es</strong>, wash → wash<strong>es</strong>, go → go<strong>es</strong>, do → do<strong>es</strong></em><br>Consonant + <em>y</em> becomes <em>-ies</em>: <em>study → stud<strong>ies</strong>, try → tr<strong>ies</strong></em><br>But vowel + <em>y</em> just takes <em>-s</em>: <em>play → plays</em>.",
        "tag": "Spelling"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>be</strong> has its own forms. Learn them by heart.<br><em>I am · you are · he/she/it is · we are · you are · they are</em><br><strong>have</strong> is almost regular. Only he/she/it is different: <strong>has</strong>.<br><em>I am Priya. She is from Leeds. He has two sisters.</em><br>Common mistake: ✗ <em>He have a car</em> → ✓ <em>He has a car</em>.",
        "tag": "be, have"
      },
      {
        "html": "For negatives and questions, use <strong>do / does</strong> + the base verb. <strong>does</strong> already shows the -s, so the main verb gets no -s.<br><em>I don't work. She doesn't work. Do you work? Does she work?</em><br>✗ <em>She don't work</em> → ✓ <em>She doesn't work</em><br>✗ <em>Does she works?</em> → ✓ <em>Does she work?</em><br>With <em>be</em> there is no do: <em>She isn't tired. Are you tired?</em>",
        "tag": "do / does"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>always, usually, often, sometimes, never</strong> go <strong>before</strong> the main verb but <strong>after be</strong>.<br><em>She always walks to work. I never drink coffee.</em><br><em>He is usually late. They are never at home.</em>",
        "tag": "Adverbs"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "We use the present simple for <strong>habits, routines and facts</strong>: <em>I get up at seven. Zofia teaches maths. The shop opens at nine.</em> For things that are happening right now, use the <a href=\"/present-continuous\">present continuous</a>.",
